 text+='Production reconstruction uses fixed-cost guided **SMC**, not arbitrary-deal rejection. A combinatorial dynamic program samples the current uniform deck permutation conditioned on necessary future chance-slot and source-availability constraints within a valid unshuffled segment. Its exact valid/total multinomial ratio is the importance correction. Opening/turn-one draws can be guided jointly across intervening prize placement. Failed openings condition the complete exposed returned hand before shuffling/redealing. Reconstructed list mass is game-start mass × whole-history normalizing estimate; within-list resampling retains accumulated evidence. Compatible hypotheses are not declared impossible when allocations die. Normalized SMC remains approximate.\n\n'
 text+='The ordered stress case observes seven unique cards in order from12: the old rejection acceptance is1/3,991,680. The new conditioned random-draw kernel returns this exact evidence factor across all three seeds without waiting millions of trials. The unequal-list toy recovers the0.375 posterior rather than restoring0.5. Joint hand-and-future-deck availability also matches its exact4/6 reference.\n\n'
 text+='BaselineA uses the frozen prior after hard public composition/location compatibility. BaselineB changes only list masses to valid current full-tracker masses. Both use the same exact current-allocation combinatorics for revealed serial domains, singleton known locations, opening Basic predicates and hidden Basic slots, under their stated exchangeability assumptions. Unknown-copy presence is P(hand count > certified known count), not P(any copy). Joint subset/location bounds retain facts such as at most one previously known deck copy entering the hand on one hidden draw. Counts advance for every event within a callback; an emptied looking zone is resolved before a later draw. When an identity count is legally known but its serial is uncertain, that count is still excluded from the unknown-copy label. If full current masses are unavailable, B-plus-fallback isA and is labeled accordingly. Baseline inability to represent a hard fact stops development rather than fabricating a prediction.\n\n'
 text+='## Remaining limitations and interpretation\n\nFinite guided-SMC depletion occurs in longer compatible histories under the frozen budgets. That is neither deck contradiction nor recovered full inference: A fallback and support counts are explicit, including for the rest of the match. Unrecognized concealed deck selections, unresolved effect-specific order, private ordered-block returns such as Ciphermaniac, and identity transforms have explicit unsupported handling. No “not played means absent” inference is used. Availability-only inference plus uniform unobserved choices is an approximate model, not the opponent’s true strategic policy.\n\n'
